From: MacGameVideoFix <https://github.com/MathiasKowoll/MacGameVideoFix>
Subject: [PATCH] winebus.sys: EXPERIMENT -- the USB emulation answers a feature write without sending it

WHERE THIS CAME FROM

MacGameVideoFix's own series, mgvf-NNNN, written on 2026-09-08, the seventh of
ours and the sixth in the controller-bus set. Like mgvf-0007 it is a follow-up
to mgvf-0005 and does not apply without it: it changes that patch's own feature
path, in the emulation and nowhere else. Nothing in it is specific to this
project.

THIS IS AN EXPERIMENT AND NOT A FIX

Every patch before this one in the set repairs something that was measured to
be wrong. This one does not. It changes what the emulation does with one class
of traffic so that the next trace answers a question, and it is written to be
undone from the registry rather than by a rebuild. It should be read as an
instrument, and the section at the end says what it does not establish.

WHAT IT IS TESTING

Whether a feature-report WRITE is what makes a DualSense on Bluetooth leave a
session that mgvf-0005 is presenting as wired.

WHAT IS MEASURED

In emulation-224719.log on 2026-09-08 a DualSense Edge on Bluetooth, presented
as USB 054c:0df2 from 651695.444, was driven for 39 seconds and then the link
ended. The last exchanges, all inside 104 milliseconds:

  651734.111  feature 0x09 read, 20 bytes, answered
  651734.135  feature 0x20 read, 64 bytes, answered
  651734.142  feature WRITE, report 0x08, 48 bytes, "08 02" and then zeros,
              to which mgvf-0005 appended the Bluetooth CRC e0 ef a2 23
  651734.185  feature 0x05 read, 41 bytes, ANSWERED BY THE PAD
  651734.192  output report 0x02, translated and sent
  651734.193  output report 0x02, translated and sent
  651734.215  IOKit: device removed

macOS's own log for that instant says the pad initiated the parting: "Received
disconnection indication on device DualSense Edge Wireless Controller reason
431". Not a link failure and not macOS letting go of the device: the pad chose
to leave.

That log contains exactly ONE feature write. Against it stands
steam-hid-game-145815.log -- the same Edge, on Bluetooth, under Steam, written
output reports from 623849.520 to 630997.316, about two hours -- which contains
NOT ONE. Across every session recorded so far, the pad left within seconds of
Sony's libScePad driving it and stayed when only Steam did, and the feature
write is the one piece of traffic that separates the two.

The audio-request theory that produced mgvf-0007 is REFUTED and that patch's
own header says so: in the working session Steam sent the same audio bit twice
and the pad stayed for another 900 seconds and more after each. mgvf-0007
stands on its own narrower ground -- the request is for hardware the pad has
only on a cable -- and is not the cause of anything here.

THE CHANGE

In the emulation path only, a feature-report WRITE is ANSWERED AS IF IT HAD
SUCCEEDED and no byte of it reaches the pad. The client's IoStatus receives
STATUS_SUCCESS and Information the length the client offered, which is what a
wired pad's successful write returns.

ANSWERED, NOT REFUSED. That distinction is the whole design and it is load
bearing. libScePad builds this report in its feature sender at 0x1800074b0 --
image base 0x180000000, read with llvm-objdump from the copy one current title
ships -- which writes byte 0 as the report id (0x08 for a pad that is not a
Bluetooth DualShock 4), byte 1 as 0x02, sends 0x30 bytes through HidD_SetFeature
(the tenth entry of the HID.DLL import table at RVA 0x1F000, that is
0x18001f048), and returns 0x8001002b when that call comes back FALSE. Its
caller is at 0x180003de6, inside the library's per-device READER THREAD -- the
function at 0x1800038d0, which is what it passes to its thread starter at
0x180014ac4, where CreateThread is called at 0x180014b24, and whose first act
is SetThreadPriority(GetCurrentThread(), 15). At 0x180003deb that caller tests
the return and, on a negative one, jumps to 0x180004263, which frees the
preparsed data, calls CloseHandle on the device handle at [dev+0x10], clears
the pad's slot in the library's device table and zeroes its 0x21f8-byte record;
the thread then returns at 0x1800043fc. So REFUSING the write would have that
library drop the pad by itself, within milliseconds, and the session would end
for a reason we introduced. Nothing would be learnt about the pad. Answering
successfully leaves the client believing what it believed on a cable and takes
only the bytes off the wire.

HOW TO UNDO IT WITHOUT A REBUILD

  HKLM\System\CurrentControlSet\Services\winebus\Devices\<vid>/<pid>
      ForwardFeatureWrites  REG_DWORD  absent (the default): the write is
                                       answered and not sent. Non-zero: the
                                       write goes to the pad exactly as
                                       mgvf-0005 sends it, CRC tail and all.

The same key mgvf-0005's UsbEmulation and ProductId and mgvf-0006's
SeizeDevice live under, named after the pad's REAL ids in lower-case hex with a
slash. ABSENCE MEANS SWALLOW, deliberately: the experiment has to be what a
fresh install does, because an experiment that must be switched on is an
experiment nobody runs, and the value exists so that a session can be put back
to mgvf-0005's behaviour between two launches without another build.

It is read the way mgvf-0005 reads UsbEmulation -- get_device_option in main.c,
which opens the device's subkey under the driver key -- and not the way
mgvf-0006 reads SeizeDevice. mgvf-0006 had no choice: its decision is made in
bus_iohid.c, in the unix half, where the registry is not reachable, so the
value has to ride struct device_options across the boundary. This decision is
made in the PE half, in the same file and the same function as the option that
turns the emulation on, so it uses the same reader and is read at the same
moment, when the pad arrives.

It is read BEFORE the two lines that rewrite the desc, and that placement is
the point: mgvf-0005's ProductId may replace the pad's product id with another
DualSense's, and a read after that would look for this pad's option under the
other pad's key while UsbEmulation and SeizeDevice were read under the real
one. The answer is carried to the write path as one bit of the report-fixups
word, which is the only thing about the pad besides its desc that
bus_create_hid_device is given, and it is set only together with
HIDRAW_FIXUP_DUALSENSE_USB.

THE SCOPE

Feature WRITES, and nothing else.

Feature READS are untouched: mgvf-0005's rule still applies, the CRC tail of a
read is still zeroed so the read looks wired, and the calibration report 0x05 is
still exempt from it. That matters here, because the pad answered such a read
after the write this patch takes away, and the experiment would be worthless if
the reads changed too.

Output reports are untouched: a 0x02 from the client still goes out as the
0x31 the pad wants, with its sequence byte and its CRC, and mgvf-0007 still
takes the audio fields out of it on the way.

A pad the emulation is not presenting is untouched entirely. The decision
function is given that condition as its first argument rather than assuming it,
so that the scope is an assertion in the host test and not only a sentence
here. A client that knows the pad is on Bluetooth and writes to it itself is
not reached by any of this.

The pure half is in dualsense_usb.c, with the reasoning, and usb_emu_test.c
beside the descriptors in the build directory exercises it: swallowed with
nothing set, forwarded for any non-zero value, and forwarded always on a device
the emulation is not presenting.

One TRACE line per swallowed write, naming the report id and the length, in the
shape of the "appended the Bluetooth CRC to feature report id ... length ..."
line it replaces, so that the next +hid trace shows plainly which of the two
happened. TRACE and not WARN, for mgvf-0007's reason: nothing has gone wrong,
this is what the build is now for.

NOT ESTABLISHED

THAT THE FEATURE WRITE IS WHAT MAKES THE PAD LEAVE. It is not, and the same
trace argues against it: 43 milliseconds AFTER the write the pad answered a
feature read, which is not the behaviour of a device that has already decided
to go, and two ordinary output reports went out after that -- 50 and 51
milliseconds after the write, with the pad gone at 73. What is measured is a
correlation between the presence of a feature write and a session ending
within seconds, over a small number of sessions, all of which are also the
sessions libScePad was driving. The write is the one thing unique to them, not
a demonstrated cause.

WHAT REPORT 0x08 IS FOR is not established either. Its content in the fatal
trace is a report id, the byte 0x02, and 46 zeros; libScePad's sender writes
exactly those two bytes and leaves the rest of the buffer as it was allocated.
Nothing on this disk says what the pad does with it, and this patch does not
claim the pad dislikes it -- only that it is the traffic that separates the
sessions.

WHETHER SWALLOWING CHANGES ANYTHING is the question, and it is open until a
trace answers it. Two outcomes are worth naming in advance. If the pad stays,
the write is implicated and the next step is to narrow which write. If the pad
still leaves at the same point, the write is cleared and the difference lies
somewhere else in what libScePad does -- and this patch should come back out.

WHAT A CLIENT LOSES by being told a write succeeded that did not is not
measured. Any state the pad would have taken from report 0x08 it does not take,
and a client that reads back what it wrote would see the difference; nothing in
the traces does that. The one thing this patch is sure of is that the client
does not stop for it, which is the whole reason it answers rather than refuses.

None of this has run against a live pad yet. As with mgvf-0005 and mgvf-0007,
the first use belongs in a +hid trace.

---
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_usb.c
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_usb.c
@@ -368,4 +368,66 @@
 
     memset(report + len - DUALSENSE_CRC_TAIL, 0, DUALSENSE_CRC_TAIL);
     return len;
+}
+
+/* MGVF mgvf-0008: whether a client's feature WRITE reaches the pad at all.
+ *
+ * AN EXPERIMENT, NOT A FIX. What it tests is whether a feature write is what
+ * makes a DualSense on Bluetooth leave a session that mgvf-0005 is presenting
+ * as wired.
+ *
+ * WHAT IS MEASURED. In emulation-224719.log on 2026-09-08 a DualSense Edge on
+ * Bluetooth, presented as USB, was driven for 39 seconds and then the link
+ * ended. The last exchanges: feature 0x09 read at t=651734.111, feature 0x20
+ * read at .135, a feature WRITE of report 0x08, 48 bytes, content "08 02" and
+ * then zeros, at .142 -- to which mgvf-0005 appended the Bluetooth CRC
+ * e0 ef a2 23 -- a feature 0x05 read at .185 which the pad ANSWERED, two
+ * output reports at .192 and .193, and IOKit reporting the device removed at
+ * .215. macOS's own log for that instant says the pad initiated the parting:
+ * "Received disconnection indication on device DualSense Edge Wireless
+ * Controller reason 431". Not a link failure and not macOS letting go: the pad
+ * chose to leave. That log holds exactly one feature write. In
+ * steam-hid-game-145815.log -- the same pad on Bluetooth under Steam, written
+ * to from 623849.520 to 630997.316, about two hours -- there is not one.
+ *
+ * WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED. That the write is what makes the pad leave. The
+ * pad answered a feature READ 43 milliseconds after it, which is not the
+ * behaviour of a device that has already decided to go, and two ordinary
+ * output reports went out after that, 50 and 51 milliseconds after the
+ * write, before the pad was gone at 73. The correlation is between libScePad
+ * driving the pad and the pad leaving; the feature write is the one thing
+ * unique to those sessions, not a demonstrated cause.
+ *
+ * WHY ANSWERED AND NOT REFUSED. This distinction is the whole design. Sony's
+ * libScePad builds that report in its sender at 0x1800074b0 (image base
+ * 0x180000000, the copy one current title ships): report id 0x08, byte 1 set
+ * to 0x02, 0x30 bytes, sent with HidD_SetFeature. Its caller is the library's
+ * per-device reader thread, and at 0x180003deb a negative return from that
+ * sender jumps to 0x180004263, which frees the preparsed data, closes the
+ * device handle, clears the pad's slot and zeroes its 0x21f8-byte record --
+ * the thread then returns. Refusing the write would therefore have that
+ * library drop the pad itself, which measures nothing about the pad. So the
+ * write is answered as a successful write of every byte the client offered,
+ * and the bytes go nowhere.
+ *
+ * THE SCOPE. Feature WRITES only. Feature reads are untouched -- mgvf-0005's
+ * CRC-tail rule still applies to them, with report 0x05 still exempt -- and so
+ * are output reports, which still go out as the 0x31 mgvf-0007 masks. A pad
+ * that mgvf-0005 is not presenting is not this experiment's business at all,
+ * which is what the first argument says: main.c reaches this only for an
+ * emulated device, and saying it here as well is what lets the host test check
+ * it.
+ *
+ * HOW TO UNDO IT WITHOUT A REBUILD. forward_option is the ForwardFeatureWrites
+ * value read from the pad's own key under
+ * HKLM\System\CurrentControlSet\Services\winebus\Devices\<vid>/<pid>, where
+ * mgvf-0005's UsbEmulation and mgvf-0006's SeizeDevice live. Its ABSENCE means
+ * swallow: the experiment is what a fresh install does, because a build that
+ * has to be asked to run the experiment would not be run. Any non-zero value
+ * puts the write back on the wire exactly as mgvf-0005 sends it. */
+unsigned int dualsense_usb_feature_write_action(int emulated, unsigned int forward_option)
+{
+    if (!emulated) return DUALSENSE_FEATURE_WRITE_FORWARD;
+    if (forward_option) return DUALSENSE_FEATURE_WRITE_FORWARD;
+    return DUALSENSE_FEATURE_WRITE_SWALLOW;
 }
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_usb.h
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_usb.h
@@ -83,6 +83,17 @@
 #define DUALSENSE_FEATURE_CALIBRATION      0x05
 #define DUALSENSE_FEATURE_CALIBRATION_LEN  41
 
+/* MGVF mgvf-0008: what the USB emulation does with a client's feature WRITE.
+ *
+ * AN EXPERIMENT, NOT A FIX. dualsense_usb.c says at length what it is testing
+ * and what has not been established; the short of it is that every session in
+ * which the pad left within seconds is a session in which a feature report was
+ * written to it, and no session in which it stayed has one. Swallowing is what
+ * happens by DEFAULT, so that the next trace is the measurement; the registry
+ * value below puts the write back on the wire without a rebuild. */
+#define DUALSENSE_FEATURE_WRITE_FORWARD 0
+#define DUALSENSE_FEATURE_WRITE_SWALLOW 1
+
 /* The CRC-32 the pad checks over Bluetooth runs over one seed byte and then
  * the report up to the last four bytes, where it is stored little-endian. */
 #define DUALSENSE_CRC_TAIL                 4
@@ -111,6 +122,19 @@
                                           unsigned char seq, dualsense_crc32_fn crc32,
                                           unsigned char *audio_dropped);
 
+/* MGVF mgvf-0008: DUALSENSE_FEATURE_WRITE_FORWARD when the client's feature
+ * write is to go to the pad as mgvf-0005 sends it, CRC tail and all, and
+ * DUALSENSE_FEATURE_WRITE_SWALLOW when it is to be ANSWERED SUCCESSFUL without
+ * a byte of it reaching the pad. Not refused: answered. dualsense_usb.c says
+ * why that distinction is the whole experiment.
+ *
+ *   emulated       whether this device carries mgvf-0005's USB emulation. A
+ *                  pad that is not being emulated always forwards.
+ *   forward_option the ForwardFeatureWrites value under the device's own key,
+ *                  or 0 when the key carries none -- absence and 0 are the
+ *                  same answer, as they are for mgvf-0005's UsbEmulation. */
+unsigned int dualsense_usb_feature_write_action(int emulated, unsigned int forward_option);
+
 /* In place. Each returns len when the report was changed and 0 when it was
  * left as it was: too short to carry a CRC tail at all, or, for the read, the
  * calibration report, whose tail a wired pad returns as well. */
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/main.c
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/main.c
@@ -83,6 +83,17 @@
  * leaves it alone -- a client's first write is not a reason to stop, it is
  * the reason the emulation exists. */
 #define HIDRAW_FIXUP_DUALSENSE_USB 0x4
+
+/* MGVF mgvf-0008: the client's feature WRITES reach the pad. Set from a
+ * per-device registry value when the device arrives, and CLEAR IS THE DEFAULT:
+ * without the value a feature write is answered successful and sent nowhere.
+ * That is an experiment and dualsense_usb.c says what it is testing; the bit
+ * is what turns it off again without a rebuild. It is only ever set together
+ * with HIDRAW_FIXUP_DUALSENSE_USB -- the experiment is the emulation's bill
+ * and has no meaning on a pad that is not being presented as wired -- and
+ * hidraw_disable_report_fixups leaves it alone for the same reason it leaves
+ * that one alone. */
+#define HIDRAW_FIXUP_DUALSENSE_USB_FEATURE_WRITE 0x8
 
 struct device_extension
 {
@@ -434,6 +445,8 @@
          * already says USB, so the Bluetooth fixups below cannot also fire. */
         TRACE("Enabling USB emulation for Bluetooth DualSense device %p\n", device);
         ext->report_fixups |= HIDRAW_FIXUP_DUALSENSE_USB;
+        /* MGVF mgvf-0008: carried with it, never on its own. */
+        ext->report_fixups |= report_fixups & HIDRAW_FIXUP_DUALSENSE_USB_FEATURE_WRITE;
     }
 
     if (desc->is_hidraw && (desc->bus_type == BUS_TYPE_BLUETOOTH) && is_dualshock4_gamepad(desc->vid, desc->pid))
@@ -599,6 +612,7 @@
 static UINT32 dualsense_usb_emulation_fixups(struct device_desc *desc)
 {
     unsigned int length;
+    UINT32 fixups;
     DWORD present;
 
     if (!desc->is_hidraw || desc->bus_type != BUS_TYPE_BLUETOOTH) return 0;
@@ -622,9 +636,24 @@
 
     TRACE("presenting Bluetooth DualSense %04x:%04x as USB %04x:%04lx, %u-byte report descriptor\n",
           desc->vid, desc->pid, desc->vid, present, length);
+
+    /* MGVF mgvf-0008: read here, before the two lines below, because this is
+     * the last point at which the desc still carries the pad's REAL product
+     * id. "ProductId" may replace it with another DualSense's, and a read
+     * after that would look for this pad's option under the other pad's key --
+     * while UsbEmulation and SeizeDevice, which this value sits beside, were
+     * read under the real one. Absence means swallow; see dualsense_usb.c. */
+    fixups = HIDRAW_FIXUP_DUALSENSE_USB;
+    if (dualsense_usb_feature_write_action(TRUE, get_device_option(desc->vid, desc->pid, L"ForwardFeatureWrites", 0))
+        == DUALSENSE_FEATURE_WRITE_FORWARD)
+        fixups |= HIDRAW_FIXUP_DUALSENSE_USB_FEATURE_WRITE;
+    TRACE("feature writes from the client will be %s\n", (fixups & HIDRAW_FIXUP_DUALSENSE_USB_FEATURE_WRITE) ?
+          "sent to the pad, because ForwardFeatureWrites is set for it" :
+          "answered without being sent, which is mgvf-0008's default");
+
     desc->bus_type = BUS_TYPE_USB;
     desc->pid = present;
-    return HIDRAW_FIXUP_DUALSENSE_USB;
+    return fixups;
 }
 
